{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And YHWH said to Moses: Yet one plague</b> — This was said to him while he stood before Pharaoh, for once he had gone out from before him, he never again saw his face — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 11:4). And so our Rabbis said (Exodus Rabbah 18:1): the Holy One, blessed be He, sprang upon him — as it were, He entered the palace of Pharaoh for Moses' sake, because he [Moses] had said, \"I shall never again see your face,\" so that he [Pharaoh] would not find him to be lying. And you find that HaKadosh Baruch Hu did not speak with Moses in Pharaoh's house except at that [one] hour, as it is written, \"as I go out of the city\" (Exodus 9:29); but now HaKadosh Baruch Hu sprang and spoke with Moses, as it is said, \"Yet one plague,\" and so forth.<br>Now here too the narrative is abbreviated, for YHWH said to him, <b>Yet one plague I will bring upon Pharaoh</b>, and He informed him of that plague, and said to him, \"At midnight I go out into the midst of Egypt\" (Exodus 11:4), and the entire content of that passage (Exodus 11:4–8); but Scripture did not wish to elaborate on the saying that HaShem spoke to Moses, for it is sufficient what Moses recounted to Pharaoh — \"Thus says YHWH\" — as I explained in the section of אַרְבֶּה (Ramban on Exodus 10:2). And many passages in the Torah come in this manner and according to this pattern: the section of \"Sanctify to me every firstborn\" (Exodus 13:2) is abbreviated in HaKadosh Baruch Hu's address to Moses, and elaborated in Moses' discourse to the people, \"Remember this day\" (Exodus 13:3), and the conclusion of the entire passage — and they are the words of HaShem to Moses, which he spoke to Israel in the very language he was commanded."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:06:01.102307960Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}